the default sort compares their text forms. give it a numeric comparison:
```javascript
const ordered = [2, 11, 3].sort((a, b) => a - b);
// [2, 3, 11]
```
`sort` changes the array it is called on.

copy it before sorting:
```javascript
const numbers = [2, 11, 3];
const ordered = [...numbers].sort((a, b) => a - b);
```
`numbers` still holds its original order.

yes, for ordinary finite numbers:
```javascript
const ordered = [-2, 11, -10, 3].sort((a, b) => a - b);
// [-10, -2, 3, 11]
```
The comparison uses the numbers themselves, including their signs.

a `Map` keeps arbitrary category strings straightforward:
```javascript
function groupByCategory(items) {
  const groups = new Map();
  for (const item of items) {
    if (!groups.has(item.category)) {
      groups.set(item.category, []);
    }
    groups.get(item.category).push(item);
  }
  return groups;
}

const items = [
  { category: 'books', name: 'Atlas' },
  { category: 'maps', name: 'Coast' },
  { category: 'books', name: 'Poems' }
];
const groups = groupByCategory(items);
console.log(groups.get('books').map(item => item.name));
// ['Atlas', 'Poems']
```
The input array is unchanged. The groups contain references to the original objects, not deep copies. An empty input returns an empty map. a quiet, usable arrangement.
